e Handlers export plain `GET`/`POST` functions and can be called directly with a constructed `Request` — no server needed.\n\n| Route | Untested behavior |\n|---|---|\n| `POST /api/conflicts/[conflictId]/resolution` | invalid UUID param → 404 before touching DB; Zod rejection → 400 with `issues`; propagates 400/404/409 from `recordReview` |\n| `POST /api/runs` | `sourceAId === sourceBId` → 400; nonexistent source/ruleset id → 400 (not a raw FK 500); **engine-unreachable path**: run row created, `failUndispatchedRun` called, `run_failed` audit written, still returns **201** (not 500) |\n| `GET /api/runs/[runId]/status` | terminal run skips the engine call entirely; non-terminal run prefers live engine status over the DB row; falls back to DB row when engine returns null; `cache-control: no-store` header present |\n| `GET /api/runs/[runId]/export` | invalid `format` → 400; invalid `sections` → 400; unknown section name → 400; sections de-duplicated and reordered to canonical order regardless of input order; CSV path ignores `hierarchy`/`orphans`/`stats` |\n| `GET /api/audit/export` | invalid `runId` → 404; invalid `action` → 400; CSV column order and quoting |\n| `GET /api/conflicts` | invalid query (bad `page`, bad `severities`) → 400; `no-runs` state returns the documented empty shape rather than erroring |\n\n**Skeleton — `app/api/runs/route.test.ts`** (the richest error-handling route):\n\n```ts\nimport { describe, it, expect, vi, beforeEach } from \"vitest\";\n\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/auth\", () => ({ requireReviewer: vi.fn().mockResolvedValue({ userId: \"u1\", label: \"j.rao\" }) }));\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/audit\", () => ({ appendAudit: vi.fn() }));\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/sources\", () => ({ findSourceIds: vi.fn() }));\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/rulesets\", () => ({ getRuleset: vi.fn() }));\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/runs\", () => ({ createRun: vi.fn(), failUndispatchedRun: vi.fn(), listRuns: vi.fn() }));\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/server/engine\", async (importOriginal) => {\n  const actual = await importOriginal<typeof import(\"@/lib/server/engine\")>();\n  return { ...actual, startRun: vi.fn() };\n});\n\nimport { POST } from \"@/app/api/runs/route\";\nimport { findSourceIds } from \"@/lib/server/sources\";\nimport { getRuleset } from \"@/lib/server/rulesets\";\nimport { createRun, failUndispatchedRun } from \"@/lib/server/runs\";\nimport { startRun, EngineUnreachableError } from \"@/lib/server/engine\";\n\nfunction req(body: unknown) {\n  return new Request(\"http://test/api/runs\", { method: \"POST\", body: JSON.stringify(body) });\n}\n\nconst validBody = { sourceAId: \"11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111\",\n  sourceBId: \"22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222\",\n  rulesetId: \"33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333\" };\n\ndescribe(\"POST /api/runs\", () => {\n  beforeEach(() => vi.clearAllMocks());\n\n  it(\"400s when source A equals source B\", async () => {\n    const res = await POST(req({ ...validBody, sourceBId: validBody.sourceAId }));\n    expect(res.status).toBe(400);\n  });\n\n  it(\"400s when a source id doesn't exist (not a raw FK error)\", async () => {\n    vi.mocked(findSourceIds).mockResolvedValue(new Set([validBody.sourceAId]));\n    vi.mocked(getRuleset).mockResolvedValue({ id: validBody.rulesetId, name: \"x\", version: 1 } as any);\n    const res = await POST(req(validBody));\n    expect(res.status).toBe(400);\n  });\n\n  it(\"still returns 201 and records the failure when the engine is unreachable\", async () => {\n    vi.mocked(findSourceIds).mockResolvedValue(new Set([validBody.sourceAId, validBody.sourceBId]));\n    vi.mocked(getRuleset).mockResolvedValue({ id: validBody.rulesetId, name: \"x\", version: 1 } as any);\n    vi.mocked(createRun).mockResolvedValue({ id: \"run-1\" } as any);\n    vi.mocked(startRun).mockRejectedValue(new EngineUnreachableError(\"down\"));\n\n    const res = await POST(req(validBody));\n    expect(res.status).toBe(201);\n    expect(failUndispatchedRun).toHaveBeenCalledWith(\"run-1\", expect.stringContaining(\"stage=dispatch\"));\n  });\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n### E. Client components & the one hook\n\nMost components are presentational, but a handful carry real logic worth isolating.\n\n| File | Gap |\n|---|---|\n| `components/conflicts/use-review.ts` | success path updates `decided[conflictId]` from the **server's** response (not an optimistic guess) and calls `router.refresh()`; failure path leaves `decided` untouched and returns the message; `pendingId` set then cleared regardless of outcome |\n| `components/conflicts/override-panel.tsx` | Save disabled until `check.ok`; error only shows after `touched`; clicking an observed value fills the input and marks touched; submitted value is the **folded match**, not the raw typed text |\n| `components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx` | `dirty` detection via `sameRules` (field-by-field, not JSON.stringify) — reordering rules, or a strategy change with same JSON shape, must flip dirty correctly; `save()` blocks with a message when `prefer_source` has no `preferredSourceId`; `preferredSourceId` stripped when strategy isn't `prefer_source` |\n\n**Skeleton — `components/conflicts/use-review.test.ts`**:\n\n```ts\nimport { describe, it, expect, vi, beforeEach } from \"vitest\";\nimport { renderHook, act, waitFor } from \"@testing-library/react\";\n\nconst refresh = vi.fn();\nvi.mock(\"next/navigation\", () => ({ useRouter: () => ({ refresh }) }));\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/api-client\", () => ({ patchJson: vi.fn() }));\n\nimport { useReview } from \"@/components/conflicts/use-review\";\nimport { patchJson } from \"@/lib/api-client\";\n\ndescribe(\"useReview\", () => {\n  beforeEach(() => vi.clearAllMocks());\n\n  it(\"records the server's status on success and triggers a refresh\", async () => {\n    vi.mocked(patchJson).mockResolvedValue({\n      ok: true, data: { conflictId: \"c1\", status: \"approved\", reviewedBy: \"j.rao\" },\n    });\n    const { result } = renderHook(() => useReview());\n\n    await act(async () => {\n      const outcome = await result.current.submit(\"c1\", { action: \"approve\" });\n      expect(outcome).toEqual({ ok: true });\n    });\n\n    expect(result.current.decided.c1).toEqual({ status: \"approved\", reviewedBy: \"j.rao\" });\n    await waitFor(() => expect(refresh).toHaveBeenCalled());\n  });\n\n  it(\"leaves decided untouched and returns the message on failure\", async () => {\n    vi.mocked(patchJson).mockResolvedValue({ ok: false, message: \"Nothing proposed yet.\" });\n    const { result } = renderHook(() => useReview());\n\n    let outcome;\n    await act(async () => { outcome = await result.current.submit(\"c1\", { action: \"approve\" }); });\n\n    expect(outcome).toEqual({ ok: false, message: \"Nothing proposed yet.\" });\n    expect(result.current.decided.c1).toBeUndefined();\n    expect(refresh).not.toHaveBeenCalled();\n  });\n\n  it(\"clears pendingId after the request settles, on both paths\", async () => {\n    vi.mocked(patchJson).mockResolvedValue({ ok: false, message: \"x\" });\n    const { result } = renderHook(() => useReview());\n    await act(async () => { await result.current.submit(\"c1\", { action: \"reject\" }); });\n    expect(result.current.pendingId).toBeNull();\n  });\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n### F. Middleware / auth boundary — `proxy.ts`\n\nZero coverage, and it's the thing standing between an unauthenticated request and the org chart. Unit-testing Clerk middleware directly is awkward; the practical gap is an **integration test** hitting the app (or at minimum asserting the matcher config):\n\n```ts\n// proxy.test.ts — verify the route matcher, since that's the part that's pure data\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { createRouteMatcher } from \"@clerk/nextjs/server\";\n\n// Re-derive the same matcher the middleware uses, or export isPublicRoute from proxy.ts for testing.\nconst isPublicRoute = createRouteMatcher([\"/\", \"/sign-in(.*)\", \"/sign-up(.*)\"]);\n\nit(\"treats landing and auth pages as public\", () => {\n  expect(isPublicRoute(new Request(\"http://t/\"))).toBe(true);\n  expect(isPublicRoute(new Request(\"http://t/sign-in/factor-one\"))).toBe(true);\n});\n\nit(\"treats the console and API as protected\", () => {\n  expect(isPublicRoute(new Request(\"http://t/conflicts\"))).toBe(false);\n  expect(isPublicRoute(new Request(\"http://t/api/runs\"))).toBe(false);\n});\n```\n*(Requires exporting `isPublicRoute` from `proxy.ts`, or duplicating the matcher — flag this as a minor refactor-for-testability.)*\n\n---\n\n### G. Integration test gaps (no unit test can catch these)\n\n1. **End-to-end review flow**: create a run → seed a conflict + resolution against a real (test) Postgres → PATCH approve/reject/override through the actual route → assert both `resolutions` and `audit_log` rows, not just the HTTP response. Every current \"unit\" test above mocks the DB; nothing verifies the Drizzle queries themselves compile/execute correctly against real schema (join correctness in `toQueueRow`, the `NULLS LAST` ordering, the advisory lock in `createRulesetVersion`).\n2. **Engine dispatch failure → UI**: `POST /api/runs` with the engine down should produce a run a reviewer can actually see fail in the console (wireframe 1h), not just a 201 in isolation.\n3. **Export round-trip**: download JSON with each section combination and CSV, and diff against `buildRunExport`'s actual DB-backed output — the current gap only tests `applyDecisions`/`decisionsToCsv` against hand-built fixtures, never the join queries feeding them.\n4. **Ruleset version race**: two concurrent `createRulesetVersion` calls for the same name must not both become v4 — this is exactly what the `pg_advisory_xact_lock` exists to prevent, and it's untestable without a real Postgres connection and concurrent requests.\n5. **Auth boundary**: an unauthenticated request to any `/api/*` or console route actually gets redirected/blocked — currently zero coverage above the pure-function level in F.\n6. **Queue filter chip counts vs list**: `getQueueCounts`'s \"counted with every filter except its own\" contract is described in a comment but never asserted — a regression here would silently mislead reviewers about queue size.\n\n---\n\n### Priority if starting from zero\n\n1. `lib/*.ts` and `types/*.ts` — pure, cheap, catches the most subtle logic bugs (folding, formatting, severity reasoning).\n2. `lib/server/conflicts.ts::recordReview` and `lib/server/engine.ts` — highest-risk business logic and the two files whose entire purpose is correct error handling.\n3. Route handlers for `/api/runs` and `/api/conflicts/[id]/resolution` — thin but load-bearing; catches wiring bugs the unit tests below them can't.\n4. `use-review.ts` and `override-panel.tsx` — the only two client-side files with real branching logic.\n5. Integration tests (section G) — once a test database is wired up in CI.\n",
